(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-1b-1c-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Class IB & IC Antiarrhythmics Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which history is a listed contraindication to lidocaine?","choices":["Glaucoma","Heart block","Seizures","Asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Seizures are a listed contraindication to lidocaine, whose own central nervous system toxicity can end in seizures. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which automaticity do class IB drugs decrease?","choices":["That of ectopic foci","That of the sinoatrial node","That of atrial pacemakers","That of the atrioventricular node"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class IB drugs shorten phase 3 and decrease ectopic foci automaticity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which change in the action potential is most marked with class IC drugs?","choices":["A shortened phase 3","A prolonged phase 3 alone","A much slower phase 0","A flatter phase 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The class IC tracing shows a markedly slower phase 0 upstroke; class IC slows phase 0 and conduction and prolongs the refractory period. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which serious risk is listed for flecainide after a myocardial infarction?","choices":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Increased mortality","Lupus-like syndrome","Cinchonism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide's toxicity list includes increased mortality in myocardial infarction, which is why it is kept for hearts without significant structural damage. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Propafenone shares flecainide's mechanism and adds which activity?","choices":["Alpha-blocking activity","Potassium channel activation","Calcium channel blocking activity","Beta-blocking activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Propafenone has the same mechanism as flecainide plus beta-blocking activity, which brings bradycardia, bronchospasm and worse heart failure. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram intervals does flecainide prolong?","choices":["PR and QRS","PR, QRS and QT","QRS and QT","PR and QT"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide prolongs the PR, QRS and QT intervals; it blocks both sodium and potassium channels in the ventricles. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which effect on the cardiac action potential defines class IB?","choices":["Markedly slowed phase 0","Prolonged phase 3","Flattened phase 4","Shortened phase 3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Class IB shortens phase 3, so the action potential ends sooner, with an unchanged upstroke; it also decreases ectopic foci automaticity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of propafenone comes from its beta-blocking activity?","choices":["Agranulocytosis","Blurred vision","Bronchospasm","Proarrhythmia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Propafenone's toxicity list pairs bronchospasm with beta-adrenergic blockade; bradycardia and worsening heart failure come from the same activity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Lidocaine toxicity shows mainly in which system?","choices":["Central nervous system","Pulmonary system","Thyroid gland","Hematologic system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine's toxicity is central nervous system toxicity: drowsiness, confusion, restlessness, muscle twitches, seizures, and nystagmus as the early sign. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Flecainide is to be used only in which patients?","choices":["Those without structural heart damage","Those with a prior myocardial infarction","Those with depressed cardiac function","Those who are slow acetylators"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide is to be used only in patients without significant structural damage to the heart, and it increases mortality after myocardial infarction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which nervous system effects are listed for flecainide?","choices":["Tinnitus and dizziness","Nystagmus and muscle twitches","Neuropathy and ataxia","Blurred vision and tremor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide's toxicity list starts with blurred vision and tremor, and also includes seizures. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Besides sodium channels, which channels does flecainide block in the ventricles?","choices":["L-type calcium channels","Beta receptors","Alpha receptors","Potassium channels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide blocks sodium and potassium channels in the ventricles and slows recovery of the sodium channel. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which finding is an early sign of lidocaine toxicity?","choices":["Seizures","Nystagmus","Tinnitus","Corneal deposits"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nystagmus is listed as the early sign of lidocaine toxicity, within its central nervous system toxicity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
